# Caldera Core — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Core, the Holdfast the whole game defends — a
broad stepped brass keep, a ziggurat of receding tiers rising to a crowned summit, with
banners hanging from its upper tiers and a beacon at the very top — as a 3D voxel model
with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells
one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably as this seat of power,
keeps itself alive on its own, and grinds up into a taller, prouder keep when it is
upgraded. There is no target model to copy. This is the biggest, most detailed built
thing of its roster — spend the volume on it.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Core is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a tiered keep whose banners stir and
whose tiers rise needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent
the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the keep, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the keep's front face looks toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front). Up is +y.
- The Core is a **static building founded on the ground**: its broad base sits flat on the
  ground at `y = 0` and does not move. Build it symmetric about the lengthwise vertical
  centerplane — the tiers, the crown, and the beacon centered on it, the banners mirrored
  left and right.
- The Core is deliberately **broad and monumental** — it fills most of the width and depth
  at its base and steps inward as it rises to a crowned summit. It is by far the LARGEST
  built thing on the field; nothing about it should read as small, slight, or machine-like.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Core (a tier already stacked
  on the tier below it, a banner already hung on its upper tier, the beacon already at the
  summit).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, pipework, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto the accent at run time to show the Core's upgrade
level (see *The accent region*); baking any of them in makes a Core that has not yet been
upgraded look permanently upgraded.

## What the Core is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Core must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **broad stepped brass keep**: a ziggurat of receding tiers, wide at the base and
  stepping inward as it rises, founded flat on the ground and reading as heavy masonry
  architecture — a seat of power, not a machine. Its shadowed seams and undersides are
  brass dark.
- A **crowned summit** at the top of the tiers, ringed by a crown of ironwork or battlement
  — the seat the beacon stands on.
- **Banners** hanging from the upper tiers, mirrored left and right, that stir on their own
  (see the animations).
- A **beacon** standing at the very top of the summit that turns on its own — the highest
  point of the whole model.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions, how many tiers
the keep steps through and how each is massed, how the crown rings the summit, how the
banners hang, how the beacon is shaped, and how you break the keep into rig parts. Nothing
here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably
the Core and animates convincingly.

The Core is the **Holdfast the whole game defends — monumental masonry architecture, not a
machine**. The tell is the STEPPED TIERS, the hanging BANNERS, and the crowning BEACON —
it is by far the largest built thing on the field, a tiered building rather than a
machine, and nothing about it reads as small, slight, or machine-like.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the Core's **upgrade level**, by finding
every voxel of one reserved color and repainting it (brass dark at level 0, then steel,
then gold, then white-hot at the top level), so one model serves all four levels. That
reserved color is the **verdigris accent** `#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract
work:

- Sculpt the **crown ring at the summit** and the **trim edging the banners** — and only
  those — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- Each accent element must be a **clean, unbroken run** — the crown a continuous ring
  around the summit, the banner trim a continuous edging on each banner — and clearly
  **visible from more than one angle**, so a player reads the Core's upgrade level at a
  glance across the battlefield.

Everything else — the tiers, the masonry, the beacon body, the recesses — keeps its own
color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and every Core in the game looks the same
level.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a slow, living stir at idle and a weighty, grinding rise on upgrade.

- **`idle`** — the life of the keep (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`,
  `auto_play = true`). The beacon at the summit turns and the banners hanging from the
  upper tiers stir, cycling on their own with no caller, so the Core reads as alive and
  garrisoned even when nothing is happening. Only the beacon and the banners move; the
  tiered keep beneath them stays fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`upgrade`** — becoming more powerful (a game-triggered ONE-SHOT, `loop = false`). It
  plays **once and holds its final pose**, not a loop: the keep's tiers grind and rise, the
  crown ring lifts and locks, and the beacon flares — a single visible, satisfying moment
  of the Core becoming more powerful, played the instant the player pays for an upgrade. At
  the end the keep stands taller and prouder and **holds** that stronger pose; it does not
  snap back to its start.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a slow
drift of smoke from the beacon), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle`
and `upgrade` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the tiers stack cleanly and step
inward, the crown rings the summit unbroken, the banners hang on their tiers, the beacon
stands at the very top, and the animations read with the right weight. Run `voxel-anim
render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry your result is built
from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render --component <part>` renders
one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the model posed at
that instant to check the motion).
